<div class="interview">


<div class="question">
<h3>1. Where did the idea for <em>Do Not Come</em> come from?</h3>
<p>
The idea came from a Spring Break trip taken with friends through Mexico in the early 1990s. Some events were inspired by things that happened during that trip.
<br><br>
After staying at a run-down hotel, we ended up at a resort in the middle of nowhere, almost entirely full of women at a convention. We were practically the only foreigners there, and the night got wild.
<br><br>
Because we were isolated and surrounded by Mexican staff who seemed wary of us, I had the feeling that the looks weren't particularly friendly. Maybe it was the tequila.
<br><br>
I remember thinking: "We're on our own. We'll get out of here because they want us to. But what if they decide they don't?"
<br><br>
That feeling of vulnerability was the real starting point. I began writing the sense of danger almost without stopping. Reality gave me the starting point; fiction took it from there.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>2. Did anything from the trip actually make it into the novel?</h3>
<p>
Yes. Some uncomfortable and tense moments did. Fortunately, we all made it home safely.
<br><br>
Over time, memories mixed with imagination and found their way into the novel. Reality was the starting point; fiction took it from there.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>3. The novel revolves around three friends. Why three?</h3>
<p>
Those three grew out of the trip. My travel companions were friends and college classmates. Crazy things happened, but we returned safely.
<br><br>
Years later, I wondered what would happen if three friends were placed in a much more extreme situation.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>4. You have lived and worked in different parts of the world. How did that influence the novel?</h3>
<p>
Mainly through the settings and the way I approached different countries and characters.
<br><br>
Living in different places gives you a firsthand sense of different ways of thinking and behaving. I wanted the settings to feel believable and the characters from different countries to feel authentic.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>5. Why Mexico?</h3>
<p>
Mexico is a fascinating country, and its border with the United States made it a natural fit for the story.
<br><br>
It also had many of the ingredients I needed. And there was a personal connection: Mexico was tied to the origins of the novel.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>6. What interests you about intelligence and international operations?</h3>
<p>
I'm interested in what goes on between the United States and the rest of the Americas. Some operations work, others don't, and there are consequences for people who have nothing to do with them.
<br><br>
I'm interested in the less visible side, the consequences and collateral damage outside the official story.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>7. Which writers have influenced you?</h3>
<p>
European writers, especially Pierre Lemaitre and his approach to stories and characters. I've also read the classic espionage writers. Everything you read becomes part of what you write.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>8. How do you approach plotting?</h3>
<p>
I start with the main plot roughly mapped out. Then the side stories take on a life of their own, intersecting with the main story and sometimes taking the characters in directions I hadn't planned.
<br><br>
I enjoy that. Readers appreciate unexpected turns.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>9. How did you develop the three main characters?</h3>
<p>
They are three very different people, but close friends. I started with small personality traits. Those traits grew, and the events helped me discover who the characters really were.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>10. What was the most difficult part of the research?</h3>
<p>
The medical material. I had to do a lot of research to make it believable. Technical subjects require actual research. You have to make sure that what you're describing makes sense.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>11. Which part of the novel did you enjoy writing the most?</h3>
<p>
Probably the New York section. Places and scenes have their own atmosphere, and New York was one of them.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>12. How much research did you do into intelligence and geopolitics?</h3>
<p>
I've always been interested in geopolitics and intelligence, so I was already curious about those subjects. For the book, I spent almost a year doing intensive research. I needed to understand certain aspects before incorporating them.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>13. The characters are placed under extreme pressure. Why?</h3>
<p>
Because pressure outside your comfort zone reveals who you are. It reveals values, limits and decisions. I'm interested in what happens when people are forced to confront situations they never imagined.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>14. What made you interested in the survival instinct?</h3>
<p>
I first saw the appeal while watching <em>Black Hawk Down</em>. There's something powerful about putting people in situations where they have to improvise and act on survival instinct. Pressure reveals who they are.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>15. What do you hope readers take away from <em>Do Not Come</em>?</h3>
<p>
I hope they feel their time was well spent and that they got something from the book. I want them to be entertained, but also left with something to think about. I want it to feel like a journey.
<br><br>
If they finish thinking, "Wow," even better.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>16. When did you first start writing?</h3>
<p>
I started at school. Sometimes we were punished with stories about saints. The sooner you finished, the sooner you could play.
<br><br>
Whenever the subject was free, I wrote fiction. I could write a story in five minutes. That's probably where it started.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>17. Your writing has a cinematic quality. How much does cinema influence you?</h3>
<p>
I love movies, but movies are expensive and budgets start making decisions for you.
<br><br>
Writing is the closest thing to being God. There's no budget and no limit. You can create a character, take them anywhere and make almost anything happen.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>18. What have you learned about the writing process?</h3>
<p>
Not many rules. You can build characters and situations without following the usual genre rules and the story can still work.
<br><br>
The important thing is that characters feel alive and have personalities. Then they sometimes do things that surprise you.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>19. What has been the most rewarding part of writing the novel?</h3>
<p>
Characters can stay with someone for a while, sometimes for the rest of their life.
<br><br>
With <em>Do Not Come</em>, Fred generates the most debate. Readers want to talk about him. That's a good sign: the character has made it off the page and into the reader's mind.
</p>
</div>


<div class="question">
<h3>20. Is <em>Do Not Come</em> simply a thriller, or is there something more?</h3>
<p>
It's always more. There's social criticism, black humor, and characters who aren't what they seem.
<br><br>
The thriller is the vehicle. Underneath, there's a look at people, ambitions, decisions and consequences. I'm interested in characters under pressure and in confronting things they never thought they were capable of.
</p>
</div>

</div>
</div>


